      "claim": "The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a pragmatic, non-inductive rule of action based on verisimilitude (truth-likeness), not probability.",
      "mechanism": "Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which remains unproven and improbable) and its epistemic standing after severe testing. A theory that survives severe tests has demonstrated a higher degree of 'verisimilitude'—it has withstood attempts to falsify it where others failed. Choosing it is not an inductive inference that it will hold in the future, but a rational decision to act on the hypothesis that has currently best approximated truth by surviving the most rigorous criticism. The mechanism is 'survival of the fittest' applied to ideas: we prefer the survivor not because we predict its future survival (induction), but because it is the only one currently standing.",
      "counterconditions": [
        "If 'verisimilitude' itself implicitly relies on an inductive assumption that past resistance to falsification correlates with future resistance.",
        "If the definition of 'severe test' secretly embeds probabilistic expectations about the world.",
        "If acting on a theory necessarily implies believing it is more likely to be true, collapsing the distinction between action and belief."
